The Scroll of Scare Monster actually far precedes Elbereth.  It was an
item in Rogue (Nethack's predecessor) and was notable in that game in
that reading it wasted the item, but dropped on the ground it became
one of the most useful items in the game.  Unlike Nethack these days,
in Rogue a player usually had to utilize every trick he found in a game
just to survive, and Scare Monster was a way to survive arbitrarily
long against almost all opponents (all except Dragons, who could
breathe fire) and build experience.  It was also ultimately limited by
the player's food supply.

In Rogue, Scare Monster was the only scroll that worked this way, and
it had no (or at best a very short-lived) effect if read.  The idea, as
best I can figure, is that the runes on a scroll of Scare Monster had
special significance, which is not a concept completely alien to
systems of magic in pen-and-paper RPGs.

There isn't a compelling gameplay reason to make other scrolls work
this way too, but the idea that items can be used in ways other than
reading them is interesting.  It's not something that makes sense for
all scrolls, but for certain examples (like scrolls of fire), it's a
way to provide uses for items that would otherwise be seen as "bad."
It is a very Nethackish thing to be able to turn a "bad" item into a
useful one, as it is to find an ingenious, unexpected use for
something.

Ultimately, the justification for wielded and thrown scrolls working
like this is "It's magic, of course it'd work in ways you wouldn't
expect."  That's ultimately just a justification for doing whatever we
want to the poor game, but at least there's a weird kind of logic to
it, and it's not wholly without precedent.

